WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.200
What do you really know about&nbsp;
how the Dutton family came to be?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.000
What guest stars are actually doing&nbsp;
their real-life job on the show?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.720
Ever think Gator might actually&nbsp;
be doing the cooking off-screen?

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.960
"Yellowstone" fans have Taylor Sheridan&nbsp;
to thank for bringing the series to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.520
Sheridan is the one who first&nbsp;
began setting the groundwork for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.440
what would become "Yellowstone" way back&nbsp;
in 2013. If his name is ringing a bell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.280
it's probably because he's one of Hollywood's&nbsp;
biggest up-and-coming, behind-the-scenes talents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.520
After a short career as an actor that saw&nbsp;
him taking on a small part on "Veronica Mars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.360
and a recurring role as Deputy Chief of&nbsp;
Police David Hale on "Sons of Anarchy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.120
Sheridan decided to move behind the camera.&nbsp;
In fact, it was his weariness of acting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.360
turned him to screenwriting in the first place,&nbsp;
thus motivating him to create "Yellowstone."

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.160
Sheridan is best known for his first two&nbsp;
efforts as a screenwriter. 2015's "Sicario,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.320
a visceral tale of an FBI agent's descent&nbsp;
into the world of Mexico's cartel wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.480
was met with widespread acclaim. His follow-up,&nbsp;
the modern western "Hell or High Water," nabbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.080
him an Oscar nomination for Best Original&nbsp;
Screenplay. He also wrote and directed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.400
2017 film "Wind River" before successfully&nbsp;
pitching his idea for "Yellowstone."

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.360
The impetus for "Yellowstone" came&nbsp;
from a desire to tell a story about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.840
gentrification in the American west. In an&nbsp;
interview with Deadline, Sheridan explained,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.160
"It is the most American of us, the West, and&nbsp;
land developers sell that fantasy. And people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.320
who can afford the fantasy are very, very&nbsp;
wealthy people from LA to New York, Dallas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.120
and Florida... In the process, those land values&nbsp;
and inheritance taxes are killing a way of life."

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.600
One of the most significant factors that&nbsp;
draws viewers to "Yellowstone" is its star:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.240
Oscar award winner Kevin Costner. It's the&nbsp;
multi-talented actor's first time starring on a TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.040
series—besides the limited miniseries "Hatfields&nbsp;
&amp; McCoys." Sheridan viewed Costner’s involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.840
as an integral part of setting the show's&nbsp;
tone and style. He told Variety in June 2018,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.680
"Kevin's one of the biggest movie stars of&nbsp;
the past 40 years, and well deserved. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.840
an incredible storyteller as a director, as a&nbsp;
writer, as an actor, and so when you have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.680
kind of tool in your toolbox, you can write&nbsp;
him into some really conflicting situations."

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.720
“Having a conversation with you is&nbsp;
like a martian talking to a fungo.”

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.720
As it turns out, there's a reason Costner&nbsp;
has been slow to make the leap to TV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.800
He told IndieWire in 2019 that he's had&nbsp;
difficulty feeling comfortable creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.240
a character without knowing what their&nbsp;
full arc was. He described the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.480
as a more vulnerable way to go through life&nbsp;
as an actor, even going so far as to say,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.680
"It hasn't been an easy adjustment&nbsp;
for me ... I don't like it too much."

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.040
Despite his misgivings about working in&nbsp;
television, Costner has been effusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.440
in his praise for Sheridan. In an interview with&nbsp;
Collider, the actor said of Sheridan's work ethic,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.480
"He's had to pay a lot of dues, and&nbsp;
a lot of things — he's cleaned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.480
a lot of horse stalls in Hollywood, so to speak."

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.360
As with any massive TV production, things&nbsp;
haven't always gone smoothly behind the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.800
of “Yellowstone.” According to Deadline,&nbsp;
the show hit its first roadblock before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.120
its pilot had even aired, and only months after&nbsp;
Paramount Network had greenlit the production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.800
For the first season of "Yellowstone," Paramount&nbsp;
Network partnered with the Weinstein Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.600
who agreed to produce the series. However, in&nbsp;
October 2017, The New York Times and The New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.000
Yorker published reports of the Weinstein Company&nbsp;
co-founder Harvey Weinstein's history of sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.880
harassment and abuse. Swift action was then&nbsp;
taken to distance Weinstein from "Yellowstone."

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.080
They released a statement declaring that&nbsp;
Weinstein's name would be removed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.360
the credits for both "Yellowstone" and&nbsp;
another co-produced show for the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.560
titled "Waco." The president of&nbsp;
Paramount Network, Kevin Kay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.360
also stood firm in their decision during a press&nbsp;
tour. As reported by Deadline, the exec explained,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.720
"There are hundreds of people who worked on&nbsp;
both "Waco" and "Yellowstone"... and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.160
people shouldn't be penalized... That has&nbsp;
nothing to do with them and we want a safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.600
workplace. Nobody wants to be associated&nbsp;
with the things that went on there."

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.280
When pressed about Weinstein's involvement&nbsp;
on "Yellowstone" and "Waco," Kay added,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.840
"I want to say definitively that Harvey Weinstein&nbsp;
has never been part of the creative process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.760
Until a new name of the company is announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.560
Weinstein Co. will not be listed&nbsp;
in the credits for either show."

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.080
The primary dramatic focus of "Yellowstone" is on&nbsp;
the white Dutton family. However, their proximity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.240
to the fictional Broken Rock Indian Reservation&nbsp;
and ongoing feud with tribe chairman Thomas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.400
Rainwater means that Native American characters&nbsp;
are featured prominently on the show as well.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.000
Controversy erupted over the casting of&nbsp;
actress Kelsey Asbille in the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.520
Monica Long Dutton — a Native American&nbsp;
woman and the wife of one of the Dutton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.480
sons. Asbille had previously claimed to be of&nbsp;
Chinese and Cherokee descent; Schön! Magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.600
described her in a July 2018 profile as part&nbsp;
Cherokee and half Chinese, presumably because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.640
that's what she said during the interview. A&nbsp;
November 2018 profile of Asbille in W Magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.280
also described her as being of Chinese, English,&nbsp;
and Cherokee descent. She told the publication,

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.480
"It's been a blessing to get to explore&nbsp;
native culture [on "Yellowstone"].&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.480
As a person of mixed race, as you get older it&nbsp;
matters more to you who you are and where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.480
come from. So to be able to get in touch with&nbsp;
that side of my heritage has been amazing."

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.680
However, according to native&nbsp;
news source Pechanga.net,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.360
Asbille’s assertions might not be as&nbsp;
accurate as she would have us believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.520
When her claims were presented to the Eastern&nbsp;
Band of Cherokee Indians — the tribe Asbille&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.800
claimed her lineage descended from — they&nbsp;
responded with a statement declaring,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.440
"No documentation was found&nbsp;
in our records to support any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.480
claim that she descends from the&nbsp;
Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians."

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.400
Outside of the casting controversy, several&nbsp;
Native American people involved in the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.480
production have spoken highly of their&nbsp;
experiences. Veteran actor Gil Birmingham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.680
told Indian Country Today that he was&nbsp;
thrilled to be on the show, saying,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.560
“One of the highlights is to have a&nbsp;
prominent Native American character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.400
in a contemporary piece that's empowered&nbsp;
and not playing a victimization portrayal."

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.160
He also praised Sheridan's handling of&nbsp;
native issues, but clarified by adding,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.880
"It would be nice if we could get&nbsp;
some more Native writers included,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.840
but getting work for as many&nbsp;
people as we can is a good start."

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.760
“Our first fight is against being ignored.”

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.720
AJ Not Afraid, chairman of the Crow Nation,&nbsp;
whose reservation stands in for the fictional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.560
Broken Rock Reservation, also praised the&nbsp;
portrayal of contemporary Native Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.680
in "Yellowstone." He told Variety that&nbsp;
when it came to the representation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.840
Native American characters, "Yellowstone"&nbsp;
is a lot more realistic than other shows.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.880
In 2018, animal rights advocacy group PETA&nbsp;
claimed that a whistleblower who worked on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.960
the set of "Yellowstone" informed them that&nbsp;
the show used actual dead bovines in a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.960
that depicted mutilated cow carcasses.&nbsp;
They characterized this as both wasteful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.560
and having the potential to expose the&nbsp;
cast and crew to disease. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.160
the whistleblower noted that the carcasses&nbsp;
were left out in the sun for a long period of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.480
time during filming and produced a stench that&nbsp;
was difficult for the crew to later wash away.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.680
Paramount Network denied this claim&nbsp;
shortly after it became public. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.200
company's SVP of communications,&nbsp;
Kurt Patat, said in a statement,

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.160
"Paramount Network takes animal&nbsp;
safety very seriously and with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.320
utmost professionalism [...] The production has&nbsp;
taken necessary precautions to provide for animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.960
safety and their well-being on set. All animals&nbsp;
are monitored on set by professional handlers. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.640
have been in touch with PETA which presented&nbsp;
us with inaccurate claims that we were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.640
to correct including no cows were killed&nbsp;
or mutilated for the scene in question."

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.560
Thankfully, this is the only claim of&nbsp;
animal cruelty that "Yellowstone" has faced.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.880
Just because "Yellowstone" centers on&nbsp;
family and political conflict doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.440
that filming hasn’t gotten intense. Speaking&nbsp;
with Parade Magazine, actress Kelly Reilly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.000
who plays the smart and scary Beth Dutton,&nbsp;
described a particularly harrowing day on set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.920
She explained that she had to film a scene where&nbsp;
her character runs toward a pack of wolves during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.880
a very unorthodox date. The scene involved Beth&nbsp;
yelling at said pack of wolves as they attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.040
to feed, which only intensified her worries&nbsp;
about everything going south. Reilly said,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.080
"They were real wolves, they were&nbsp;
tamed, but they were still real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.080
wolves. They had like invisible fishing line&nbsp;
on them with two people lying in the grass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.280
so if they had charged me... I kept&nbsp;
saying to them, 'You really think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.720
that's going to stop them?' And they were&nbsp;
like, 'Well, we can't do anything else.'"

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.000
In another incident, Reilly described&nbsp;
how she almost fell victim to a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.920
Google Maps recommendation during a break&nbsp;
from filming. While attempting a day trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.440
the British actress took a shortcut and&nbsp;
found herself on a precarious Montana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.520
mountain road in the middle of December in&nbsp;
an ill-equipped car. She told Parade that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.760
her first reaction when returning to level&nbsp;
ground was of shock and gratitude, saying,

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.280
"I can't believe I survived."

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.440
Following the finale of "Yellowstone"&nbsp;
Season 3, an interviewer for Deadline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.040
asked Taylor Sheridan how members of his&nbsp;
cast took to horseback riding. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.280
Sheridan himself appears skillful on horseback&nbsp;
during his cameo appearances on the show. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.720
revealed that Beth Dutton actor Kelly&nbsp;
Reilly is the most experienced rider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.760
though viewers might not know this&nbsp;
given that, of the main players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:55.920
her character spends the least amount of time on&nbsp;
a horse. Jimmy Hurdstrom actor Jefferson White,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:00.080
meanwhile, had to learn how to ride a horse&nbsp;
more or less from scratch. Sheridan said,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.920
"We had him out there two or three days in&nbsp;
a row... horseback riding is all about trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.520
between horse and rider. It takes a lot of&nbsp;
trust for someone who has never been on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.000
horse to get on one. And the horse can feel&nbsp;
that fear so it was pretty dicey for a bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.720
To his credit, the second day I&nbsp;
had him out there, just riding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.400
never complaining. And when he got off,&nbsp;
he said, I'll fix this, I promise."

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.200
“Don’t worry, Jimmy, it will all be over soon.”

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.200
Some of the ranchers featured on "Yellowstone,"&nbsp;
meanwhile, are professionals in their real lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:30.000
A video shared by Paramount Network to its YouTube&nbsp;
channel details how real-life cowboy Jake Ream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.160
started working behind-the-scenes of "Yellowstone"&nbsp;
before he was offered an on-camera role. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.720
character, who was simply named Jake, allowed him&nbsp;
to finally show off his wrangling skills on TV.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.840
Dedicated viewers of "Yellowstone" know that&nbsp;
the Dutton family employs a cook named Gator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.480
Perhaps most famously, Gator makes Beth a smoothie&nbsp;
consisting of three scoops of ice cream and three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.040
shots of vodka, per her specifications. As&nbsp;
it turns out, Gator is portrayed by a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.360
actually nicknamed Gator, with the birth name&nbsp;
Gabriel Guilbeau. In addition to bringing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.840
real-life nickname to the character, Gullibeau is&nbsp;
likewise an experienced chef who is responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.240
for many of the meals served to the "Yellowstone"&nbsp;
cast and crew. In a profile of the man Paramount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.320
Network shared to its YouTube channel, Jimmy&nbsp;
Hurdstrom actor Jefferson White claimed,

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.400
“Anybody on the crew, anybody in the cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.440
can't talk about this show for five&nbsp;
minutes without mentioning Gator."

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:19.120
Actor Ian Bohen described Guilbeau's&nbsp;
presence on-set as essentially a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.960
restaurant open from crew call to wrap&nbsp;
where you can have whatever you want.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.880
Though Guilbeau was born near Santa Barbara,&nbsp;
California, he grew up in Louisiana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.640
which influenced his style of cooking.&nbsp;
On his personal Instagram account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.480
Guilbeau often shows off some of the meals he&nbsp;
cooks during the filming of "Yellowstone," like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.160
for example, a pair of whole pigs he&nbsp;
served to its cast and crew. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.560
recently headed craft services for the&nbsp;
FX on Hulu comedy "Reservation Dogs."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:46.320
He posted a few different meals he cooked up&nbsp;
for them during the making of that series too.

